---
/**
 * Side-by-side sample contour maps: settlement vs heave.
 * Illustrative only — not a real property.
 */
---

<section class="survey-samples" aria-labelledby="survey-samples-heading">
  <h2 id="survey-samples-heading">What a floor survey looks like</h2>
  <p class="lead">
    Same kind of map. Two different stories. Reading the pattern — settlement vs heave — is part of
    what you pay for.
  </p>

  <div class="survey-pair">
    <figure class="survey-figure">
      <img
        src="/images/sample-settlement.svg"
        alt="Sample floor elevation contour map showing a settlement pattern with a relative low area"
        width="480"
        height="360"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        <strong>Settlement</strong> — floors drop toward a low. Often edge or area support loss.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="survey-figure">
      <img
        src="/images/sample-heave.svg"
        alt="Sample floor elevation contour map showing a heave pattern with a relative high area near the center"
        width="480"
        height="360"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        <strong>Heave</strong> — floors rise toward a high. Common with moisture-sensitive clay.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <p class="survey-note">
    These are <em>sample</em> maps for education — not a real address. A real visit uses your floor
    plan and measured points. Pattern alone is not a diagnosis; context and limits still apply.
    <a href="/disclosure/">See scope &amp; disclosure</a>.
  </p>
</section>
